 apis  um so let's let's have a look at an  example then so here's alice she's using  some kind of client and she's got an  oauth 2 access token  that she's approved with the  messages.read scope here  and then she makes a request to the api  to read her messages he's using this  access token  so she makes a get request to slash  users slash alice messages with his  access token or rather her client does  on her behalf  and gets back her messages  but then what if her client or her just  changes the alice in that in that uri to  to some other user id like bob and it's  quite common for the uris that you're  accessing an api to have  to have identifiers in them that that  are very easy to guess you can you can  guess somebody else's user identifier or  you know uh let's say it's a document  and it's using the database id which is  just a sequential number so you can  easily guess other other ids for other  documents  so you know  if we look at this if we just look at  the scope then the scope says  messages.read and it doesn't really say  it should only be alice's messages that  she can read and so if we're only using  the access token scope to grant access  then um we might have a problem here  and in fact people often do have this  problem and it's uh it's such a common  problem in in web application security  that it's got a special name and its  name is idor  and that stands for insecure direct  object reference  and this is the idea that  you're not really checking properly  what object somebody is trying to access  and so if they can guess the uri for a  different object  then they might be granted access to it  when they should have been denied so  here you know she changes alice to bob  in the uri  and because we're only checking that she  has the read read messages scope  then she gets back bob's messages  and this seems like a daft uh  kind of vulnerability but it's actually  so common that it was for a while it was  it had its own category in the owasp top  10. so if you're not familiar with the  owasp top 10 owasp is the open web  application security project and they  publish this top 10 periodically of the  most common web application  vulnerabilities so if you're if you're  new to application security it's well  worth kind of  uh  searching for that and having a look  so it's not the be all and end all of  security as i said but it's it gives you  an idea of what some of the most common  vulnerabilities are and what things to  look out for and this as i said this  used to be its own category it's now  been merged into a broader category of  uh broken access control  but it's it's this basic idea that if  you're not not  checking you know who has access to what  you're just saying you know something  like well they've got access to the  messages api and the read  um access  you know encoding this or2 scope then  they might be able to then read other  people's messages  so relying purely on oauth scopes for  authorization can lead to these kind of  ideal vulnerabilities because the way  that oauth scopes are typically designed  doesn't encode a lot of these extra  details about you know exactly whose  messages should be granted access to  so what are these other details  so if you look at the traditional kind  of components of making an authorization  decision  they kind of break down into these four  categories that are required to make a  good authorization decision so this is  uh information about the subject so who  is trying to perform this request who is  who is trying to get access to something  the resource what are they trying to  access is it some photos is it a  document is it you know  uh some payroll data or whatever uh what  action they're actually trying to  perform whether they're trying to read  or write or modify or delete  and then typically some environmental or  context-specific  information so you know where are they  trying to  gain access from you know are they are  they trying to access from home when  actually this is only allowed for  internal users on an internal networks  they  or are they accessing at a weird time of  day  and that feeds into some kind of  decision process that then makes is  going to make some kind of a loud denial  decision and here i'm not talking about  any specific technology for making that  decision  so this could be something like access  control lists or some kind of permission  system it could be role-based access  control or attribute based access  control or it could just be a bunch of  ad hoc rules that you've kind of hard  coded into your application but the  point is that you should be making a  decision based on  at least some of these broad categories  of  information  whereas when you try and just use oauth  scopes for this at least how they're  typically used  then you're you're throwing away a lot  of this information and you're only  getting some partial information to use  to make that request so you typically  you know the scope doesn't encode  anything about the user  typically because scopes are a static  list that you can go and read on the  documentation for an api  and they they don't typically encode the  user  uh they typically only encode part of  the resource so it will typically say  which type of resource is being accessed  but not which specific resource so you  know  if you've got a document read scope  which documents should you be able to  read  they do typically encode the action  pretty well  but they also then don't encode any kind  of context-specific  information about the request  so you're really limiting yourself if  you just use scopes  so could you encode all of that  information into scopes and i've seen  people try and do this and try and cram  every very complicated encoding schemes  where they try and cram all this  information into a scope  and in theory you know you could you  could try and do this you know strings  can be arbitrarily complicated you can  add whatever information you want but it  becomes you know much more difficult to  enforce this  and in particular when you think about  things like documents which are shared  resources that many users might have  access to  how is the authorization server supposed  to know who has access and and who  doesn't and what scopes are appropriate  and and not and the only way it's really  going to know that is by kind of  reaching into your data model behind the  scenes and kind of looking at  relationships between things and trying  to work out who should have access which  which kind of creates then a very tight  coupling between your authorization  server and the rest of your  your your applications and your apis  which can make things very brittle to  change over time  and you've also got to think about you  know what if those permissions change  after the token is issued when you issue  an access token to something like a  mobile app or something like a  tv set top box you know when you log  into netflix or something it  gets an access token that it's going to  use to access your your account  and those tokens can live for a very  long time  typically you know potentially multiple  years so  and so if you think about you know if  alice has access to this document when  when the token is issued is she still  going to have access to that document  two years down the line  because the scopes that are issued in  the token are fixed for the life of that  token  um and so um and if you try and  revoke the token uh when her permissions  change that means she's just gonna end  up having to log in again all the time  so uh so trying to encode too much into  scopes can make your application very  brittle  so there is a new specification coming  down the line which i'll just mention  briefly here it's called rich  authorization requests or ra  which is currently going through the  earth working group  which  enhances oauth with um  with this idea of authorization details  where you can pass in structured json  saying exactly what is being um  uh asked for  and in this case you know this can be  very very detailed unique  object for each transaction so it's kind  of designed for open banking and and  payment transactions and things like  that  so so in the future it might be possible  to do make these more kind of  fine-grained decisions purely based on  on this  scope thing but i would say that this  still has in my opinion some of the the  issues i just pointed out on the last  slide  so what should you be able to doing then  so um one thing you can think about  doing is layering oauth over some other  authorization mechanism so rather than  trying to do everything with scopes you  do some things with scopes and then  other things with a more traditional  authorization process  so you you yeah  a client accesses an api with an access  token you're going to validate that  access token check it's not expired or  whatever and check it was issued by  the correct authorization server  and then from that validation step  you're going to get back the the  scope of that token and who the resource  owner was and then you can check the  scope and say you know is that scope  appropriate for the re action that's  being performed against this type of  resource and if it isn't then you can  abort otherwise you go through to a more  traditional authorization decision where  you take the resource owner from that  access token so who originally approved  that access token and say you know would  they have access to this thing anyway so  if this is this is alice's access token  does she have access to bob's messages  well no so it would be denied there  where she does have access to her own  messages  and so you layer o-auth on top of an  existing authorization  process  so some key takeaways here then so so  keep it simple let's say you know  do this thing you don't try and solve  everything in oauth2 scopes  you know layer oauth on top of some  other  more fine-grained authorization  mechanism that's built into your app  itself built into your api  and and then keep it simple so follow  these these examples from google and  dropbox and github and so on because  they have converged on this common  approach  which is a kind of sweet spot for for  scope design  which is that you  use scopes only to distinguish between a  particular api or resource type  and the actions that can be performed  and you only break it down into more  detail where where that's a decision  that a user is going to care about so  particularly when sharing access to  their specific data  and avoid these idol vulnerabilities  these insecure direct object references  but making sure that you're not just  using the scope as the sole  indicator of whether whether a request  should be allowed  okay that's my takeaways for scope  design for security if you have any  questions um  ask in the chat and i'll um hang around  to take any questions thank you
 

